    "topic_code": "1.12",
    "topic_title": "Confirming Continuity over an Interval",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = √x / (x² − 4x + 3). On which of the following intervals is f continuous at every point?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "[−1, 1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This choice checks the denominator's zeros but ignores the domain of the square root. For −1 ≤ x < 0, √x is undefined, so f is not defined there and cannot be continuous there. Fix: List every domain restriction from each function family, such as roots, denominators, and logarithms, before testing an interval."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "[0, 3)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This choice excludes x = 3 but overlooks the zero at x = 1, which lies inside the interval. Since x² − 4x + 3 = (x − 1)(x − 3), f is undefined at x = 1, so it is not continuous on all of [0, 3). Fix: Factor the denominator completely and check whether each zero falls inside the interval."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "(2, 4)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This choice looks only at the endpoints. Both endpoints give defined values of f, but the interior point x = 3 makes the denominator zero, so f has a vertical asymptote inside the interval. Fix: Check every special point between the endpoints, not just the endpoints themselves, before claiming continuity on an interval."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "(1, 3)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "f is a quotient of continuous functions, so it is continuous wherever it is defined. √x requires x ≥ 0, and the denominator (x − 1)(x − 3) is zero at x = 1 and x = 3. The open interval (1, 3) contains only nonnegative values and neither excluded point."
     }
    ]
   },
